One winter, a traveler came with a bag of coins and a question. “What is worth more than gold?” he asked. The villagers looked away, for the truth was simplest and hardest: memory itself. Mara opened the lamp and poured out a whisper that hovered like frost. It spoke of a boy who learned to listen to the rain, a girl who learned to listen to the stones, a man who learned to listen to his own fear and walk through it.

The traveler laughed, counting his coins, but the whisper found him anyway. It settled on his sleeve and told him a secret: every act of listening multiplies, like light splintering through glass. He followed the whisper into the forest, and the trees leaned close, sharing what they remembered of the old highway and the last frost of the year.

When he returned, the village’s lamp burned brighter than before. The memory grew, and with it, the people remembered their names, the river’s song, and the fragile art of listening to the world.
